  {
    "title": "Uniqueness of the finite-stage descent for sheared Witt vectors",
    "description": "For a commutative ring presented as a directed union of subrings S_i with colimit the subring ⨆ S_i, it is known that every finitely-supported Witt vector over the colimit descends to a truncated Witt vector at some single stage S_i. The proposed direction is to prove the complementary uniqueness statement making the descent a bijection: if two truncated Witt vectors over stages S_i and S_j map to the same Witt vector over ⨆ S_i, then they already become equal at a common later stage S_k with i ≤ k and j ≤ k. Approach: reduce to the coordinate level, use directedness to merge the two source stages, and exploit injectivity of the subring inclusions. Establishing both surjectivity (descent) and this uniqueness realizes the colimit as a genuine bijection colim_i W_fin(S_i) ≅ W_fin(⨆ S_i), not merely a surjection.",
    "tags": ["WittVectors", "FilteredColimits", "CommutativeAlgebra"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Verschiebung filtration as the canonical truncation tower",
    "description": "The truncation tower used to exhibit sheared Witt vectors as a colimit is defined coordinatewise (vanishing beyond level n). The proposed direction is to replace it with the intrinsic Verschiebung filtration: the images V^n(W(R)) of the n-fold Verschiebung, which is a ring-theoretic ideal filtration rather than a set-level one. Conjecture: the finitely-supported Witt vectors are exactly the union over n of a suitable Verschiebung-defined complement, and the colimit identification becomes an identity of filtered abelian groups compatible with Frobenius and Verschiebung. Approach: express the coordinatewise truncation in terms of V and the Teichmüller lift, then prove the two filtrations are cofinal so they define the same colimit.",
    "tags": ["WittVectors", "Verschiebung", "Filtrations"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Sheared colimit over non-directed diagrams and the failure locus",
    "description": "The colimit identification for sheared vectors relies essentially on the index category being filtered (directed). The proposed direction is to quantify exactly how it breaks for non-filtered diagrams: given a family of subrings whose join is the whole ring but whose index poset is not directed, characterize precisely which finitely-supported vectors still descend to a single stage. Conjecture: descent holds if and only if the finite support set of a vector lies within a subfamily admitting a common upper bound, so the obstruction is measured by the nerve of the covering poset. Approach: build explicit two-generator lattice examples where a two-coordinate vector fails to descend, and relate the obstruction to a first Cech-style cohomology of the poset cover.",
    "tags": ["WittVectors", "OrderTheory", "Obstructions"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Min-plus (tropical) analogue of Frobenius on sheared vectors",
    "description": "Finitely-supported vectors over the tropical semiring Tropical(WithTop N) form the colimit of their truncations, exactly as for Witt vectors, differing only in the choice of basepoint (tropical infinity versus zero). The proposed direction is to transport the Witt-vector Frobenius and Verschiebung endomorphisms across this bridge and identify their tropical shadows: candidate operators are the coordinate p-fold dilation (tropical Frobenius) and the coordinate shift (tropical Verschiebung). Conjecture: these two tropical operators satisfy the min-plus analogue of the defining Witt relation FV = p, with ordinary multiplication by p replaced by tropical multiplication (numerical addition) by log_p-scaled shifts. Approach: define the operators on truncated tropical vectors, verify compatibility with the truncation embeddings, and pass to the colimit.",
    "tags": ["Tropical", "WittVectors", "Frobenius"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Quantitative descent depth as a measure of shearing",
    "description": "When a finitely-supported vector over a filtered colimit ring descends to a single stage, there is a least stage index at which it descends, its descent depth. The proposed direction is to study descent depth as a function of the vector and of the filtration. Conjecture: for the variable-support filtration on a polynomial ring, the descent depth of a finitely-supported Witt vector equals the maximum variable index appearing across its finitely many nonzero coordinates, and this maximum is subadditive under the Witt addition. Approach: prove the exact formula for monomial coordinates, extend by the additivity structure of Witt sums, and use it to give an effective bound on how large a truncation stage is needed to realize any given sheared vector, sharpening the purely existential colimit statement into a quantitative one.",
    "tags": ["WittVectors", "Complexity", "Filtrations"]
  }
